52
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

53
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:29,000
So there was a post on the blog last week about volunteers and the apparent lack of volunteers sometime,

54
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
And what followed was a lot of interesting comments and a little bit of a discussion thread in the comments,

55
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,000
And that's where things really happen when people start talking back and forth.

56
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Let's go over what I put up real quick, because I've often heard about the difficulty of recruiting adult help from other scouts.

57
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:57,000
When we sit down around the campfire and it's just us scouts, we get a little complaining sometimes.

58
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:05,000
How many times have you heard things like we're the only ones who will do this and we can't get anybody else to help.

59
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
And all of these other parents- how can they be so irresponsible?

60
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Well, you know, like anybody who volunteers for something, you love the work and you're always bothered by the fact, slightly, that we do it all.

61
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
I mean that's pretty understandable, isn't it?

62
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:31,000
I mean, especially if you're dedicated and really sold on the work, you're going to have a little bit of resentment about people who aren't,

63
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
And that's that's fine,

64
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Just as long as you don't let that resentment color everything else that you do. that's important,

65
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:40,000
And here's what I mean.

66
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:51,000
If we stand up in front of a group of parents and we plead for help and we get a little whiny and resentful about things, that's not going to be all that attractive.

67
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,000
I'm not going to be attracted to an appeal like that.

68
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
I'm going to be rather resistant to an appeal like that.

69
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Now, we've never really lacked for volunteers in our troops.

70
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Sometimes we seem to have almost too many.

71
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,000
We tell every income family that we'd welcome their help, and we do understand that schedules and commitments can be kind of tight.

72
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
And when folks do volunteer we make it clear we understand they'll do what they can do when they can do it.

73
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Are some more dedicated than others?

74
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Do some shoulder a bit more burden than others?

75
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Yeah, well, that's to be expected.

76
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
We do have a lot of fun though.

77
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:33,000
I mean we have a great time working together and I think this really does serve as an attractant for other people to join in.

78
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:40,000
So if your appeals for help aren't having positive results, think about the way you're appealing or not appealing,

79
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
You know, is that resentment creeping into that?

80
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Maybe you've convinced yourself that nobody really cares and no one will.

81
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
People can sense that and it may be your attitude that keeps them away.

82
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Bill Daniel contributed in the comments section for that post and this is really great.

83
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:02,000
He says: when new families join, we tell them one to pick their level of contribution,

84
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
whether it's going to be once a year, once a month, once a week or once a day.

85
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
I mean they can sign up for whatever they want.

86
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Number two: I love this.

87
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
It's a guilt-free troop.

88
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:19,000
We're not going to make you feel guilty and we're not going to whine and carry on.

89
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
It's a guilt-free troop.

90
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Hey, you say yes or no when we ask if you can help and there are no explanations or excuses needed or wanted.

91
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
You know guilt-free.

92
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
I really like that, Bill,

93
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Great, great idea.

94
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
And number three: if we ever lack what we need, we just don't do whatever it is.

95
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
You know we've not lacked for volunteers, though

96
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Sometimes we have those scheduling problems and we have to adapt to those

97
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
And sometimes, if we don't have the volunteers, oh well

98
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And that's. you know that at first sounds a little harsh, but it's a very good attitude.

99
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Bill says: as a leader. I've learned the hard way that imputing motives to others is what leads to resentments or misunderstandings.

100
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:10,000
So rather than imagining a parent thinks we're a babysitting service, I ask a few questions and that usually reveals a lot.

101
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
That's very wise, very, very wise.

102
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Don't impute motives to others without you know knowing.

103
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:22,000
Don't jump to a conclusion without knowing something about what they're dealing with.

104
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Lastly, sometimes you want to just let everybody know about a specific need.

105
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Sometimes you're pleasantly surprised by who volunteers, but often you don't end up with volunteers unless you have a direct invitation to an individual.

106
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
And I agree,

107
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,000
And that's been the experience of a lot of people and that was reflected in some of the other comments.

108
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:49,000
Larry Geiger says that we do things very similarly.

109
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:55,000
My committee chair and I tell new folks that we'll take care of everything they don't want to do,

110
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
And then we ask: what would you like to do?

111
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
That's a great take, Larry.

112
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,000
That's great.

113
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
He says what gets? that gets them thinking about the things they like.

114
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
I mean, do they like to go camping and hiking?

115
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Are they more interested in managing money and helping out administratively?

116
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:17,000
He said the secret here is that folks doing what they like to do tend to stay in the position longer,

117
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:27,000
And when you have people staying in their volunteer positions longer, the less turnover you have, and the less turnover you have, the more volunteers you will get.

118
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Stability breeds stability.

119
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:35,000
A unit that's constantly stirred by politics and people coming and going is not a place people tend to stay.

120
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:42,000
A very stable scout master and committee chair will go long ways to promote longevity and stability in the other volunteers.

121
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:50,000
It may take a little while to engender that kind of stability in a scout troop, but it will work ultimately.

122
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:56,000
And Larry adds: he says you know, by the way, I'm a little old fashioned, but we eat well.

123
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Make sure you feed your volunteers well.

124
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
That's right,

125
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
I definitely agree.

126
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:10,000
They should eat well on camping trips and there should be, you know, some good stuff going on at courts of honor and things,

127
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Absolutely.

128
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Michael Smith added to the conversation by asking this.

129
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:21,000
He says I'm in an urban area with busy parents who weren't really exposed to Boy Scouts or the outdoors as a youth

130
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
And the aren't sold on the value scouting brings to their son.

131
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
This is why they don't volunteer.

132
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
They don't understand what's in it for them and their son.

133
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,000
Now, most of the recruiting materials I see target boys and little is aimed at adults.

134
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
What is in it for volunteers?

135
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
What is in it for parents?

136
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
You know, this was my reply to Michael.

137
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,000
I said that when your boys 10 or 11, you don't understand what's about to happen.

138
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,000
There's no way that you understand this until you've gone through it.

139
00:09:51,000 --> 00:10:00,000
And what's about to happen is that his task is going to be to differentiate himself from his family and his parents.

140
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
He'll be looking for boundaries.

141
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,000
It's not tragic and awful but, boy, I'll tell you, it can be very challenging for most parents.

142
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:17,000
If you're involved in scouting with your son, you'll maintain a connection that other parents aren't necessarily going to have.

143
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:22,000
You'll see him working and playing with his friends from a perspective few parents of joy.

144
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
You'll have the other adults and scouting to help you through rough spots

145
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:35,000
And your son will respect and listen to them long after he's not quite so interested in listening to you.

146
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Remember the NASA moon missions.

147
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:46,000
If you go back that far, there was a point at which the spacecraft would end up on the dark side of the moon and go out of radio contact for three or four hours

148
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
And it was a nail biting and nervous.

149
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:53,000
And that's what happens with our kids, boys or girls.

150
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:03,000
There's this part of adolescence where their job is to do for themselves and kind of separate themselves from their family a bit and start to make their own way in the world

151
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
And they kind of go out of communication.

152
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:13,000
And we kind of bite our nails and we hope that sometime soon we'll be able to talk to them again.

153
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:18,000
It's a little frustrating to try to explain this to people who haven't been through this process.

154
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
You really can't know what I'm talking about,

155
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:26,000
But I can guarantee you this: the years you spend in scouting with your son

156
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
cement some common points of reference and experiences that you'll always share.

157
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:36,000
You'll get to spend time together, but without being in each other's way.

158
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
That is a rare opportunity through these adolescent years.

159
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
It really is.

160
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:49,000
So there are just some thoughts about volunteers and part of the conversation associated with that article at ScoutmasterCGcom.

